From 01db1039d03ec826d017977bcbb1e65ecf8c11da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brent Koogler <brkhnc@umsystem.edu>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 16:01:42 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Re-vendor jaxlib's LAPACK FFI kernels (jax-v0.11.0 ABI,
 jax-v0.11.1 sources)

The kernels changed substantially between jax 0.9.0.1 and the 0.11 series
(lapack_kernels.cc grew from 1656 to 2644 lines), and the FFI handler
signatures have to match what the pinned JAX release lowers to, so the
vendored copies are refreshed rather than carried forward.

The sources are jaxlib/cpu at jax-v0.11.1, not at the pinned jax-v0.11.0,
and that is deliberate rather than an oversight. Of the four vendored files,
lapack_kernels.h, cpu_kernels.cc, lapack_kernels_using_lapack.cc and
ffi_helpers.h are byte-identical between the two tags; only lapack_kernels.cc
differs, by 18 lines. That difference is upstream wrapping the thread-pool
fan-out of batched LAPACK calls in PLATFORM_GOOGLE, so an open-source build
runs each batch inline. Upstream's stated reason is performance regressions
and crashes from interactions with external LAPACK/BLAS implementations --
which is exactly what this plugin links against. Keeping the 0.11.1 copy
therefore keeps a cross-thread wait out of the call path, and keeps this
plugin's only difference from the previously published one its XLA base.
The handler signatures the artifact ABI depends on are identical either way.

This is a deliberate deviation from step 5 of docs/developer/bumping-jax.md,
which says to re-vendor from the pinned tag; see docs/developer/xla-fork.md.

Drops the sparse and tridiagonal handler registrations: their kernels are not
vendored here, so registering them would not link. An executable that lowers
to cpu_csr_sparse_dense_ffi or tridiagonal_solve_perturbed_ffi fails to load
with a clear 'No FFI handler registered' message.

New dependencies at this version: absl/functional:function_ref,
absl/synchronization, absl/status and @tsl//tsl/platform.
